# Canonical C-to-Simple migration registry (binary_runtime_hardening Wave 4).
# One entry per migration unit. Markdown views are projections of this file.
# Schema: doc/01_research/infra/sspec_binary/binary_sspec_rt_hardening_frozen_design_2026-08-18.md §13

c_migrations:
  - id: C-MIG-0001
    path: src/runtime/runtime_legacy_core.c
    symbols:
      - rt_crc32_text
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.nogc_sync_mut.compression.gzip.crc32_text
    simple_callers_migrated:
      - src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/database/core.spl
      - src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/database/wal.spl
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/nogc_sync_mut/compression/crc32_text_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "5 examples, 5 passed (KAT 0xCBF43926 + C-oracle differential incl. UTF-8, 2026-08-18)"
    regression_spec: test/integration/lib/database_core_spec.spl
    regression_result: "35 examples, 35 passed post-migration (2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: codegen_lane_fail_3.5x_improving — doc/08_tracking/bug/crc32_text_codegen_lane_14x_slower_than_c_2026-08-18.md
    perf_spec: test/05_perf/lib/crc32_text_c_vs_simple_perf_spec.spl
    perf_measured: >
      Interpreter lane, 2026-08-18, body_len 2090, iters 200: O(n^2)
      byte-array rebuild 1185x C; after streaming rewrite 640x C (halved).
      Interpreter lane is NOT the parity verdict (documented ~100-1000x
      envelope); spec enforces a 1000x structural ceiling that fails the
      quadratic class. Native-lane parity verdict still pending a deployed
      fixed compiler.
    fixed_perf_bugs:
      - "PERF: crc32_table() rebuilt per call — cached at module init (val _CRC32_TABLE), 2026-08-18"
      - "PERF: crc32_text built a [u8] via per-byte array copy (O(n^2), 1185x C) — now streams s.bytes() directly (640x C), 2026-08-18"

  - id: C-MIG-0019
    path: src/runtime/runtime_timestamp.c
    symbols:
      - rt_timestamp_from_components
      - rt_timestamp_add_days
      - rt_timestamp_diff_days
      - rt_timestamp_get_year/month/day/hour/minute/second/microsecond
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.time_utils (already implemented — pure Simple, same era-based civil-calendar algorithm)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      Zero product Simple callers of the rt_ forms (verified 2026-08-18:
      only ffi_gen specs and the backend symbol closure reference them).
      Remaining consumers are the Rust seed / generated boundary.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/time_utils_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "6 examples, 6 passed (epoch, leap-day, century, 400-year, pre-epoch, add/diff incl. negatives, 100-vector shared-logic differential; 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: PERF_finding_2.01x — shared_corpus=100 simple_us=9375 c_us=4657 (ratio 2.01x > 2x threshold; not fixed here, flagged only)
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering timestamp vectors (epoch, century/400-year
      leap boundaries, 2038 rollover, pre-epoch negatives, far future),
      timestamp_from_components + add_days + diff_days combined per vector,
      2026-08-18: simple_us=9375 c_us=4657, ratio 2.01x. Exceeds the plan's
      2x PERF-finding threshold; left visible in perf_evidence output per
      C-migration test standard step 1 rather than hidden.
    shared_corpus: 100
    perf_codegen: "ARTIFACT (isolated 2026-08-18) — the 3.18x codegen /
      2.01x interpreter split is a clock-probe-overhead artifact, not a
      real JIT regression on the arithmetic. Isolation harnesses (KAT-
      guarded, same binary bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple,
      mtime 2026-08-18 01:08:42, size 59620392) on this measurement pass:
      (1) bare time_now_unix_micros() back-to-back, N=10000 pairs:
      interp 0.033us/call, codegen 0.045us/call (codegen probe cost
      1.36x interp, but both sub-0.05us — negligible in isolation);
      (2) batch-timed 10000 iters of
      from_components+add_days+diff_days with ONE clock pair around the
      whole loop (no per-call probe): interp 0.095us/iter, codegen
      0.0599us/iter — codegen is FASTER, not slower, once probe reads
      are taken out of the hot path; (3) per-call-probe replica of the
      original harness shape (3 probe pairs per iteration, N=3000):
      interp 0.0593us/call, codegen 0.0371us/call — again codegen
      faster. All three isolations contradict the original 'JIT made it
      worse' reading. Root cause: time_utils has no loop/allocation, so
      its true per-call cost (tens of nanoseconds) is the same order of
      magnitude as the clock-probe/syscall cost itself and highly
      sensitive to scheduler/syscall noise on a shared, loaded box —
      exactly the regime where a probe-dominated ratio is unreliable.
      The original 265627us/83431us=3.18x number is not reproduced by a
      probe-free measurement of the same functions; treat the original
      per-call-probe methodology as unreliable for this class of
      function, not the codegen backend as regressed. Detail:
      doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_lane_still_slow_base64url_utf8_time_utils_2026-08-18.md
      finding 3, updated verdict section."

  - id: C-MIG-0013
    path: src/runtime/runtime_mcp_core.c
    symbols:
      - rt_mcp_initialize_response_text
      - rt_mcp_write_framed_text
      - rt_stdin_read_mcp_message_text
    classification: partially_dead (CORRECTED 2026-08-18, second scan incl. scripts/)
    evidence: >
      rt_mcp_initialize_response_text and rt_mcp_write_framed_text: dead
      (no consumer beyond decl/impl/link-lists). rt_stdin_read_mcp_message_text
      is LIVE: scripts/setup/refresh-rt-forward-cache.shs:64 maps
      std.mcp_sdk.transport.stdio.mcp_stdio_read_message onto it (dynamic
      dispatch invisible to direct-call greps), and
      scripts/check/validate_mcp_native_smoke.spl:320 asserts its presence.
      The original "zero callers" claim missed scripts/ — caught by the
      deletion agent's fail-closed re-verification before any change.
    replacement: NONE NEEDED (third-pass finding, 2026-08-18): the
      forward-cache row maps a symbol name (mcp_stdio_read_message) that does
      not exist ANYWHERE in the tree — the mapping is hand-authored
      aspirational text with zero consumers, and a complete pure-Simple
      framed-read already exists (mcp_sdk/transport/stdio.spl
      read_stdin_message, handling MORE framings than the C). There is no
      live call path to rt_stdin_read_mcp_message_text — only a text
      assertion in the smoke test that checks the heredoc's own text.
    deletion_plan: >
      One atomic change: delete runtime_mcp_core.c + runtime.h decls + the 3
      link/roster refs + the stale forward-cache heredoc row + retarget the
      smoke-test assertion to the real pure-Simple read_stdin_message; then
      check-c-runtime-compiles-push.shs and cargo check must PASS.
    status: deleted
    deletion_date: 2026-08-18
    deletion_note: >
      Executed 2026-08-18: deleted runtime_mcp_core.c, removed its 3 decls
      from runtime.h, removed the runtime_mcp_core.c comment reference in
      runtime_native.c, and removed it from all 4 real link/roster
      references (tools.rs build_c_runtime_library, check-runtime-symbol
      -lane-divergence.shs LANE_B_FILES, build-core-c-bootstrap-runtime
      -capsule.shs SOURCE_FILES, and the capsule's own contract test
      core_c_bootstrap_runtime_capsule_contract_spec.spl — one more real
      reference than the original 3-blocker estimate). Removed the stale
      forward-cache heredoc row (mcp_stdio_read_message ->
      rt_stdin_read_mcp_message_text) from refresh-rt-forward-cache.shs.
      Retargeted the validate_mcp_native_smoke.spl assertion to check the
      real pure-Simple read_stdin_message in
      mcp_sdk/transport/stdio.spl instead of the dead C symbol name.
      Verified via check-c-runtime-compiles-push.shs (PASS) after the
      change.

  - id: C-MIG-0016
    path: src/runtime/test/ (27 files, 3,870 lines)
    classification: conformance_oracle
    status: verified_test_only
    evidence: >
      2026-08-18 scan: no reference from any production link list
      (native_project pipeline greps clean). The only build-system consumer
      is scripts/check/build-core-c-bootstrap-runtime-capsule.shs, which
      (a) folds ~6 selfcheck sources into the capsule FINGERPRINT inputs and
      (b) compiles them into standalone SELF_CHECK_BIN executables that must
      pass during capsule verification (die on failure) — build-time
      conformance oracles, never linked into the product runtime library.
    replacement: none_needed

  - id: C-MIG-0020
    path: src/runtime/runtime_native.c, src/runtime/runtime_legacy_core.c
    symbols:
      - rt_hash_text
      - rt_str_hash (spl_str_hash)
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.hash text.hash() / rt_hash_text ABI bridge (already
      implemented in src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/src/hash.spl — same FNV-1a
      64-bit offset basis 0xcbf29ce484222325 and prime 0x100000001b3)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      9 files declare `extern fn rt_hash_text(s: text) -> i64` and call it
      directly (hashmap.spl, hashset.spl, persistent_map/__init__.spl,
      daemon_sdk/protocol.spl, and 5 test_daemon app files). No source edit
      is needed: hash.spl's `pub fn rt_hash_text` ABI bridge already
      satisfies that extern symbol in native builds (see hash.spl's own
      "C ABI bridge" comment), so all 9 already run the pure-Simple
      implementation today. Rewriting the extern decls to `use
      std.hash.{rt_hash_text}` would be cosmetic only, and for
      persistent_map/__init__.spl (family nogc_async_immut) it would add a
      cross-family import of nogc_sync_mut.src.hash, which the layer
      guard flags on other higher-layer imports in this same run
      (`higher_layer_runtime_family` gc-warning) — left untouched to avoid
      an unrelated layering regression.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/nogc_sync_mut/hash_text_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "8 examples, 8 passed (FNV-1a KAT for empty string and \"abc\", C-oracle differential incl. UTF-8, boundary lengths, corruption-discrimination, 100-vector shared-logic differential; 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: parity_1.00x — shared_corpus=100 simple_us=86003 c_us=85656 (ratio 1.00x, no PERF finding)
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering text vectors (length 0..99, byte classes
      incl. UTF-8 multibyte via seeded LCG), 2026-08-18: simple_us=86003
      c_us=85656, ratio 1.00x. Within the plan's parity band, no fix needed.
    shared_corpus: 100

  - id: C-MIG-0021
    path: src/runtime/runtime_native.c
    symbols:
      - rt_string_to_int
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.text.parse_i64 (already implemented in
      src/lib/common/text.spl:5 — s.trim().parse_int() ?? -1)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      No production `extern fn rt_string_to_int` caller was found under
      src/lib or src/app (only this spec's own test-only extern decl). The
      symbol IS registered in the interpreter's extern dispatch table
      (interpreter_extern/sffi_string.rs::rt_string_to_int_fn), and that is
      what an interpreted `extern fn rt_string_to_int` call actually reaches
      — not the C source's `strtoll(buf, NULL, 10)` at
      runtime_native.c:5093. Measured directly (bin/simple run probe):
      rt_string_to_int("42abc") == 0 and rt_string_to_int("") == 0, which
      matches the Rust shim's strict `text.trim().parse::<i64>().unwrap_or(0)`
      and contradicts strtoll's leading-numeric-prefix semantics (which
      would give 42abc -> 42). This is a real behavioral gap between the C
      *source* and what the interpreted extern path actually executes;
      filed as a caller-note here rather than a separate bug because no
      product code depends on the divergent (malformed-input) branch.
      parse_i64 uses a DIFFERENT failure sentinel (-1 vs the oracle's 0) on
      malformed/empty input, so the differential spec asserts equality only
      on well-formed decimal integers and documents (without asserting
      cross-side equality) the 0-vs-(-1) sentinel divergence separately.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/nogc_sync_mut/parse_i64_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "7 examples, 7 passed (well-formed-integer KAT + differential vectors, empty/malformed-input sentinel divergence documented not asserted-equal, 100-vector shared branch-covering bulk loop with perf evidence, corruption-discrimination, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: "measured on the 100-vector shared corpus: simple_us=2366, c_us=1487 (ratio ~1.59x, interpreter dispatch through the same process for both sides); under the 2x PERF-finding threshold, no perf finding recorded"

  - id: C-MIG-0022
    path: src/runtime/runtime_simd_utf8.c
    symbols:
      - rt_text_validate_utf8
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: pure-Simple validated_utf8_bytes_to_text_linear (already
      implemented in src/lib/common/base_encoding/utilities.spl — strict
      RFC 3629 byte-by-byte validator: rejects truncated/malformed
      continuation sequences, overlong 2/3/4-byte encodings, and UTF-16
      surrogate code points, same contract as the C
      scalar/SSE2/AVX2/NEON-dispatched validator)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_text_validate_utf8(s: text) -> bool is declared with a clean
      text-in/bool-out ABI in src/lib/common/encoding/simd_text_ffi.spl and
      is the C oracle used by this spec. No caller migration performed —
      this entry only establishes differential correctness + perf evidence
      for validated_utf8_bytes_to_text_linear as a UTF-8 validation twin;
      migrating existing extern-declared callers is left for a follow-up.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/base_encoding/utf8_validate_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "4 examples, 4 passed (KAT for empty string and ASCII, C-oracle differential incl. valid UTF-8 multi-byte/emoji, malformed-byte discrimination (lone continuation, truncated lead, overlong, surrogate, out-of-range), shared-logic bulk differential loop over 88 branch-covering vectors, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple validated_utf8_bytes_to_text_linear
      measured ~32.9x slower than the C oracle over the 88-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_micros=116329, c_micros=3536, single run, tree-walk interpreter
      via `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18). The C side is
      SIMD-dispatched (SSE2/AVX2/NEON) while the Simple side is a scalar
      byte-by-byte loop with per-position Result allocation; ratio recorded
      here per the mandatory perf-regression rule, not hidden. Not yet filed
      as a standalone bug — treat as inconclusive-fast-path evidence pending
      a JIT/native-codegen re-measurement, since `bin/simple test` hard-defaults
      to the interpreter (see .claude/rules/testing.md run-vs-test divergence
      note) and is not the engine ordinary programs run on."
    perf_codegen: "PERF FINDING (codegen lane) — ratio 16.6x, roughly half the
      interpreter-lane 32.9x but still far over the 2x threshold. Measured
      codegen-lane 2026-08-18, binary
      bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple (Rust bootstrap seed) mtime
      2026-08-18 01:08:42, size 59620392. bin/simple run +
      SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT=1, exit 0 (no strict refusal), KAT-verified before
      timing. 300 reps x 88-vector shared corpus: simple_us=6617326
      c_us=398542. Root cause: no batched-ASCII fast path (C oracle is
      SIMD-dispatched, Simple side is a scalar byte-by-byte branch cascade).
      See doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_lane_still_slow_base64url_utf8_time_utils_2026-08-18.md
      finding 2 — still open."
    perf_fix: "Added a batched-ASCII fast path to
      validated_utf8_bytes_to_text_linear
      (src/lib/common/base_encoding/utilities.spl:84): after the leading-byte
      dispatch identifies a plain ASCII byte, an inner tight `while` loop
      advances past the whole ASCII run with one cheap comparison per byte,
      only re-entering the full 2/3/4-byte leading-byte cascade on the first
      high byte. Multibyte validation logic (overlong/surrogate/truncation
      rejections) is unchanged, byte-for-byte. Re-measured codegen lane
      2026-08-18, same binary bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple
      (Rust bootstrap seed) mtime 2026-08-18 01:08:42, size 59620392.
      bin/simple run + SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT=1, exit 0, KAT-verified (88/88
      vectors Simple==C, all valid) before timing. 300 reps x 88-vector
      shared corpus: simple_us=140011 c_us=16938, ratio 8.27x (down from
      16.6x pre-fix, roughly 2x improvement). Still >2x threshold — OPEN,
      remaining gap is the lack of true SIMD batching (C oracle uses
      SSE2/AVX2/NEON; the Simple fast path is still one comparison per byte,
      just with a cheaper inner loop)."

  - id: C-MIG-0023
    path: src/runtime/runtime.c
    symbols:
      - rt_base64url_encode
      - rt_base64url_decode
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.base_encoding.base64.{base64url_encode,
      base64url_decode} (already implemented in
      src/lib/common/base_encoding/base64.spl — RFC 4648 section 5 url-safe
      alphabet, no padding; the module's own header comment already states
      it is "the pure-Simple replacement for the C extern
      rt_base64url_decode used in id_token.spl")
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_base64url_encode(input: text, len: i64) -> text and
      rt_base64url_decode(encoded: text) -> text are registered in the
      interpreter's extern dispatch table
      (interpreter_extern/crypto.rs::rt_base64url_encode /
      rt_base64url_decode, interpreter_extern/mod.rs:665-666), backed by the
      `base64` crate's URL_SAFE_NO_PAD engine rather than the C source
      itself when reached through `bin/simple test`'s tree-walk
      interpreter — same RFC 4648 section 5 semantics as
      src/runtime/runtime.c:2552-2610, so the differential spec treats
      whichever the extern call reaches as the oracle and documents the
      distinction. No production `extern fn rt_base64url_encode/decode`
      caller migration was performed in this pass — this entry only
      establishes differential correctness + perf evidence for the
      existing base64.spl implementation as a base64url twin; migrating
      remaining extern-declared callers is left for a follow-up.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/base_encoding/base64/base64url_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "7 examples, 7 passed (RFC 4648 §10 KAT vectors re-expressed url-safe, C-oracle differential round-trip incl. UTF-8 multibyte, url-safe-alphabet-no-padding assertion, single-char-corruption discrimination, determinism check, 100-vector shared-logic bulk differential loop over length 0..99 with UTF-8 multibyte every 7th char, plus exact-boundary-length similar-case coverage (0/1/2/3-byte tails + >4KB input) added with the array-accumulator perf fix, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple base64url_encode measured ~44.1x
      slower than the C oracle (interpreter dispatch to the `base64` crate's
      URL_SAFE_NO_PAD engine) over the 100-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_us=169643, c_us=3847, single run, tree-walk interpreter via
      `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18). The pure-Simple base64_encode builds
      its output via repeated `text + text` concatenation in a scalar loop,
      while the C/Rust side is a tight byte-indexed table lookup; ratio
      recorded per the mandatory perf-regression rule, not hidden. Not yet
      filed as a standalone bug — same inconclusive-fast-path caveat as
      C-MIG-0022 (bin/simple test hard-defaults to the interpreter, not the
      engine ordinary programs run on)."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering text vectors (length 0..99, ASCII plus a
      2-byte UTF-8 codepoint every 7th char via seeded LCG), 2026-08-18:
      simple_us=169643 c_us=3847, ratio ~44.1x.
    shared_corpus: 100
    perf_codegen: "PERF FINDING (codegen lane) — ratio 44.7x, essentially flat
      vs the interpreter-lane 44.1x (no JIT improvement). Measured
      codegen-lane 2026-08-18, binary
      bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple (Rust bootstrap seed) mtime
      2026-08-18 01:08:42, size 59620392. bin/simple run +
      SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT=1, exit 0 (no strict refusal), KAT-verified before
      timing. 50 reps x 100-vector shared corpus: simple_us=6155385
      c_us=137565. Root cause: two O(n) scalar `text + text` concatenation
      passes in base64_encode/base64url_encode
      (src/lib/common/base_encoding/base64.spl:49,184) — string-rebuild cost
      appears dominated by runtime string representation/allocation, not
      interpreter dispatch, so it does not shrink under JIT. See
      doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_lane_still_slow_base64url_utf8_time_utils_2026-08-18.md
      finding 1 — still open.

      POST-FIX (2026-08-18): base64_encode/base64url_encode/base64url_decode/
      _bytes_to_text rewritten to accumulate into a `[u8]`/`[text]` array via
      `.push()` and join/convert to text ONCE at the end, replacing the
      per-char `out = out + c` scalar concatenation (same binary,
      bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple, 59620392 bytes, mtime
      2026-08-18 01:08:42). Same harness shape (50 reps x 100-vector shared
      corpus, KAT-verified before timing, SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT=1, exit 0):
      simple_us=404308 c_us=11534, ratio ~35.05x (down from 44.7x) — a ~15x
      absolute reduction in Simple-side time (6155385us -> 404308us) but
      still >2x the C oracle, so this entry stays OPEN per the plan's PERF
      threshold; remaining gap is per-byte function-call/array-push/text-
      conversion overhead versus the C table-lookup-into-fixed-buffer
      approach. Found and fixed along the way: a module-level `val [u8]`
      (`ENCODE_TABLE.bytes()` hoisted to module scope) indexed and then
      `.push()`-ed into another `[u8]` array reads back CORRUPTED under the
      codegen/JIT lane (e.g. byte 65 'A' read back as 8) — a genuine JIT bug,
      not an algorithm bug; worked around by computing the encode table bytes
      as a function-local instead of a module-level `val`. See
      doc/08_tracking/bug/jit_module_level_u8_array_index_push_corruption_2026-08-18.md.
      Interpreter-lane (`bin/simple test`) perf_evidence got WORSE after this
      fix (188006/4366 before -> ~560000-870000/3000-4000 after, i.e.
      `[u8].push()` re-allocation cost dominates under the tree-walk
      interpreter even though it wins decisively under JIT) — correctness
      unaffected (spec stays 7/7 green), and per
      `.claude/rules/testing.md` the interpreter is explicitly not the engine
      ordinary programs run on, so the codegen-lane result is the one this
      fix targets.

      PINNED-CORPUS A/B CORRECTION (2026-08-18): the POST-FIX paragraph above
      compared pre-fix c_us=137565 against post-fix c_us=11534 across two
      DIFFERENT runs/corpora (per-index lengths 0..99, code review flagged the
      12x c_us swing as evidence the comparison was not apples-to-apples).
      Re-measured with ONE harness
      (bench_base64_ab.spl, scratchpad, not committed) on a SINGLE pinned
      seeded corpus (100 inputs, byte lengths 0..2000 via LCG, printable-ASCII
      LCG content), same 50 reps, same binary
      (bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple, 59620392 bytes, mtime
      2026-08-18 01:08:42), same SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT=1 bin/simple run, KAT
      (encode-match + decode round-trip) asserted before timing on both runs:
      PRE-FIX (base64.spl restored to 31e0eaa099c~1 for the run only, then
      restored back via `git checkout --`, tree verified clean via `git diff
      --stat`) simple_us=294480660 c_us=672161, ratio ~438.11x. POST-FIX
      (current lib) simple_us=14477883 c_us=448885, ratio ~32.25x. Corrected
      improvement factor on the SAME corpus: ~13.6x (438.11x -> 32.25x), not
      the previously reported 44.7x -> 35.05x (~1.28x) — the earlier ratios
      understated the fix's real impact because the differing corpora/c_us
      baselines diluted the comparison. Still OPEN (>2x threshold). See
      doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_lane_still_slow_base64url_utf8_time_utils_2026-08-18.md
      finding 1 for the full table."

  - id: C-MIG-0024
    path: src/runtime/runtime_native.c
    symbols:
      - rt_string_rfind
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.string_core.str_last_index_of (already
      implemented in src/lib/common/string_core.spl:92-104 — a pure
      byte-slice-based reverse-search loop, deliberately independent of the
      `rt_string_rfind` extern per that file's own header comment)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_string_rfind(s: text, needle: text) -> i64 is registered in the
      interpreter's extern dispatch table
      (interpreter_extern/sffi_string.rs::rt_string_rfind_fn,
      interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1892) via
      `.as_bytes().windows(needle.len()).rposition(...)` — not the C source
      directly when running under `bin/simple test` (tree-walk interpreter).
      Both the interpreter shim and the C source at
      runtime_native.c:3733-3742 implement the identical contract (empty
      needle -> subject's byte length; needle longer than subject -> -1;
      otherwise the last byte offset of needle in subject), so this spec
      differentially compares against whichever is actually reached by the
      extern call, documenting that distinction rather than assuming the C
      file itself executes. `text.last_index_of` in src/lib/text.spl still
      delegates to the extern directly (not migrated to
      str_last_index_of here) — this entry establishes differential
      correctness + perf evidence for str_last_index_of as an independent
      twin; migrating the text.spl wrapper's callers is left for a
      follow-up.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/string_core_rfind_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "5 examples, 5 passed (KAT vectors incl. empty needle/empty haystack/no-match/needle-longer-than-subject, overlapping-needle and full-match edge cases, single-char-corruption discrimination, determinism check, 100-vector shared-logic bulk differential loop over subject length 0..99 with rotating needle classes incl. empty and too-long needles, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple str_last_index_of measured ~4.4x
      slower than the C oracle (interpreter dispatch to
      `.as_bytes().windows().rposition()`) over the 100-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_us=7324, c_us=1648, single run, tree-walk interpreter via
      `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18). The pure-Simple loop builds a fresh
      slice `s[i:i+sublen]` and does a text equality compare at every
      position, while the Rust/C sides use direct byte-window comparison;
      ratio recorded per the mandatory perf-regression rule since it exceeds
      the plan's 2x threshold, not hidden. Not yet filed as a standalone
      bug — same inconclusive-fast-path caveat as C-MIG-0022/0023
      (bin/simple test hard-defaults to the interpreter, not the engine
      ordinary programs run on)."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering (subject, needle) pairs (subject length
      0..99 via seeded LCG with a repeated "ab" motif every 5th position;
      needle rotates through empty/too-long/"ab"/single-uppercase-char/
      full-subject classes), 2026-08-18: simple_us=7324 c_us=1648, ratio
      ~4.4x.
    shared_corpus: 100

  - id: C-MIG-0025
