    shared_corpus: 100

  - id: C-MIG-0026
    path: src/runtime/runtime_native.c
    symbols:
      - rt_raw_i64_to_string
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.convert.i64_to_text (already implemented in
      src/lib/common/convert.spl:253-255 — a pure string-interpolation-based
      i64-to-decimal-text conversion, independent of the
      `rt_raw_i64_to_string` extern)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_raw_i64_to_string(n: i64) -> text is registered in the interpreter's
      extern dispatch table (interpreter_extern/sffi_string.rs::
      rt_raw_i64_to_string_fn, interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1901) via
      `value.as_int()?.to_string()` — not the C source directly when running
      under `bin/simple test` (tree-walk interpreter). Both the interpreter
      shim and the C source at runtime_native.c:3102 implement the identical
      contract (decimal formatting of an i64 including sign, including the
      i64::MIN/i64::MAX boundary values), so this spec differentially
      compares against whichever is actually reached by the extern call,
      documenting that distinction rather than assuming the C file itself
      executes.

      Candidate history: rt_string_starts_with (hinted in C-MIG-0025's
      report) is NOT registered in the interpreter's extern dispatch table
      (only rt_string_ends_with/rt_string_rfind/rt_string_concat/
      rt_string_eq/rt_string_len/rt_string_new/rt_string_bytes/
      rt_string_data/rt_string_free/rt_string_builder_*/rt_string_to_int/
      rt_raw_i64_to_string/rt_string_byte_at are present) — REJECTED,
      unregistered. rt_text_to_lower_ascii vs str_to_lower: both fully
      registered, but str_to_lower/str_to_upper CRASH
      (`semantic: string index out of bounds`) on any multibyte UTF-8 input
      because they index with single-bracket `s[i]` (codepoint-indexed)
      bounded by `s.len()` (byte-length) — a genuine defect, filed at
      doc/08_tracking/bug/str_to_lower_upper_crash_on_multibyte_utf8_2026-08-18.md
      — REJECTED, a crashing replacement is not valid. rt_string_rfind vs
      str_last_index_of: correct, but already covered by C-MIG-0024 —
      REJECTED as a duplicate.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/convert_i64_to_text_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "6 examples, 6 passed (KAT vectors incl. zero/
      positive/negative/i64::MIN/i64::MAX boundary formatting,
      powers-of-ten digit-count-transition boundaries, single-digit-
      corruption discrimination, determinism check, 100-vector shared-logic
      bulk differential loop over magnitudes spanning 1..~10^18 with sign
      alternation and forced zero/i64::MIN branches, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple i64_to_text measured ~2.3x
      slower than the C oracle over the 100-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_us=6086, c_us=2647, single run, tree-walk interpreter via
      `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18). i64_to_text goes through Simple's
      general string-interpolation formatter (`\"{n}\"`) while the Rust/C
      sides call a direct integer-to-decimal routine; ratio recorded per the
      mandatory perf-regression rule since it exceeds the plan's 2x
      threshold, not hidden. Not yet filed as a standalone bug — same
      inconclusive-fast-path caveat as C-MIG-0022/0023/0024 (bin/simple test
      hard-defaults to the interpreter, not the engine ordinary programs run
      on)."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering i64 values (seeded LCG producing magnitudes
      spanning digit counts 0..19 with sign alternation, plus forced zero at
      i % 13 == 0 and forced i64::MIN at i % 23 == 0), 2026-08-18:
      simple_us=6086 c_us=2647, ratio ~2.3x.
    shared_corpus: 100

  - id: C-MIG-0027
    path: src/runtime/runtime_native.c
    symbols:
      - rt_char_from_code
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.string_core.char_from_code (already implemented
      in src/lib/common/string_core.spl:399-400, delegating to
      char_from_code_inline:204-235 — an ASCII/control-code lookup table plus
      inline UTF-8 byte math for everything else, deliberately independent of
      the `rt_char_from_code` extern per that file's own header comment
      citing doc/08_tracking/bug/char_from_code_non_ascii_unsupported_2026-07-20.md)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_char_from_code(code: i64) -> text is registered in the
      interpreter's extern dispatch table
      (interpreter_extern/conversion.rs::rt_char_from_code_fn) via
      `char::from_u32` + `.to_string()` — not the C source directly when
      running under `bin/simple test` (tree-walk interpreter). Both the
      interpreter shim and the C source at runtime_native.c:3683-3699
      implement the identical contract (a valid Unicode scalar 0..0x10FFFF,
      excluding the UTF-16 surrogate range 0xD800..0xDFFF, encodes to UTF-8;
      anything else collapses to empty text — the C runtime's own comment
      documents this as deliberate, since the ABI layer has no exception
      mechanism), so this spec differentially compares against whichever is
      actually reached by the extern call. A previously-documented concern
      in string_core.spl about the `rt_bytes_to_text` extern (used inside
      char_from_code_inline's non-ASCII path) silently returning "" under
      the SSpec `bin/simple test` evaluator was probed live 2026-08-18
      (codepoint 233 -> "\u{e9}" on both sides) and does not reproduce on
      the current binary.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/string_core_char_from_code_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "6 examples, 6 passed (KAT vectors incl. ASCII
      letters/digits/control chars, multibyte UTF-8 across all four
      encoding-length classes (1/2/3/4-byte), invalid-codepoint edge cases
      incl. negative/above-U+10FFFF/surrogate-range collapsing to empty on
      both sides, surrogate-boundary discrimination, single-codepoint
      corruption discrimination, determinism check, 100-vector shared-logic
      bulk differential loop covering every encoding-length class plus every
      rejection branch, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple char_from_code measured ~3.5x
      the C oracle (interpreter dispatch to `char::from_u32`) over the
      100-vector bulk corpus (simple_us=9939, c_us=2811, single run,
      tree-walk interpreter via `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18), above the
      plan's 2x threshold. Recorded honestly, not hidden — same
      interpreter-lane caveat as prior PERF findings (bin/simple test
      hard-defaults to the interpreter, not the JIT/native engine ordinary
      programs run on)."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering codepoints (seeded LCG cycling through all
      four UTF-8 encoding-length classes plus forced invalid-scalar classes
      at fixed moduli: negative at i % 13 == 0, above-U+10FFFF at
      i % 11 == 0, surrogate-range at i % 7 == 0), 2026-08-18:
      simple_us=9939 c_us=2811, ratio ~3.5x.
    shared_corpus: 100

  - id: C-MIG-0028
    path: src/runtime/runtime_native.c
    symbols:
      - rt_byte_char
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.encoding.byte_char.byte_char (new file
      src/lib/common/encoding/byte_char.spl — a pure-Simple UTF-8 encoder
      for the Latin-1-transparent byte-to-scalar mapping, deliberately
      independent of std.common.string_core per this round's HARD AVOID)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_byte_char(v: i64) -> text is registered in the interpreter's extern
      dispatch table (interpreter_extern/conversion.rs::rt_byte_char_fn) via
      Rust's `byte_val as u8 as char` -- a raw Latin-1 (ISO-8859-1)
      transparency mapping (byte 0x00..0xFF maps directly to the identical
      Unicode scalar value U+0000..U+00FF) followed by UTF-8 encoding -- not
      the C source directly when running under `bin/simple test`
      (tree-walk interpreter). No prior pure-Simple counterpart existed
      outside string_core.spl, so one was written:
      src/lib/common/encoding/byte_char.spl reimplements the UTF-8 byte math
      directly (1-byte encoding for 0x00..0x7F, 2-byte 0xC2/0xC3-lead
      encoding for 0x80..0xFF) using u64 bit ops, and uses `rt_bytes_to_text`
      only as a raw byte-array-to-text memcpy primitive (the same
      non-algorithmic role that extern already plays inside
      string_core.spl's own char_from_code_inline, per C-MIG-0027), not as
      a reimplementation shortcut.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/encoding_byte_char_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "6 examples, 6 passed (KAT vectors incl. ASCII
      letters/digits/space/NUL/DEL, the 1-byte/2-byte UTF-8 encoding-length
      boundary (0x7F vs 0x80), the full 2-byte Latin-1 supplement range,
      single-byte corruption discrimination, determinism check, 100-vector
      shared-logic bulk differential loop over the full 0..255 byte range
      with forced boundary values at fixed moduli, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple byte_char measured ~2.5x the
      C/Rust oracle over the 100-vector bulk corpus (simple_us=4335,
      c_us=1731, single run, tree-walk interpreter via `bin/simple test`,
      2026-08-18), just above the plan's 2x threshold. Recorded honestly,
      not hidden — same interpreter-lane caveat as prior PERF findings
      (bin/simple test hard-defaults to the interpreter, not the JIT/native
      engine ordinary programs run on)."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering byte values (seeded LCG cycling through the
      full 0..255 range, plus forced boundary values at fixed moduli:
      0x7F at i % 17 == 0, 0x80 at i % 13 == 0, 0x00 at i % 11 == 0,
      0xFF at i % 7 == 0), 2026-08-18: simple_us=4335 c_us=1731, ratio ~2.5x.
    shared_corpus: 100

  - id: C-MIG-0029
    path: src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/math.rs
    symbols:
      - rt_math_sqrt
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.math.special.sqrt_f64 (already implemented in
      src/lib/common/math/special.spl:421-427, delegating to the private
      Newton's-method kernel `_sqrt_f64` at special.spl:73-83 -- no new lib
      file was written this round; the pre-existing kernel already satisfies
      the "independent of the extern" bar, per that module's own header
      comment stating it deliberately avoids the rt_math_* SFFI externs)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_math_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 is registered in the interpreter's extern
      dispatch table (interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1580,
      math::rt_math_sqrt_fn) via Rust's hardware `f64::sqrt` -- a
      correctly-rounded IEEE-754 sqrt instruction. No C runtime counterpart
      exists (`grep -rn rt_math_sqrt src/runtime/*.c src/runtime/*.h`
      returns zero hits); this is a pure-Rust-interpreter extern, unlike
      C-MIG-0027/0028 which had a `runtime_native.c` implementation behind
      the same-named extern. `sqrt_f64` (special.spl) is 40 iterations of
      Newton's method against the same contract (x >= 0), genuinely
      independent of the hardware instruction.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/math/special_sqrt_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "6 examples, 6 passed (KAT vectors incl. perfect
      squares, zero, tolerance-based irrational-result comparison
      (Newton's-method vs hardware sqrt can disagree by a few ULPs -- not
      bit-exact, unlike the integer/text C-MIG-0027/0028 oracles), an
      EXPLICIT SEMANTIC-DIVERGENCE assertion for negative input (oracle
      correctly returns NaN per IEEE-754; sqrt_f64 is undocumented/unguarded
      for x < 0 per its own docstring and returns finite garbage instead --
      verified live: sqrt_f64(-4.0) == 6.070281514079917, NOT NaN), single-bit
      corruption discrimination, determinism check, 100-vector shared-logic
      bulk differential loop restricted to the documented non-negative
      domain with forced perfect-square boundary values at fixed moduli,
      2026-08-18)"
    c_status: no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple sqrt_f64 measured ~17.7x the
      Rust hardware-sqrt oracle over the 100-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_us=8109, c_us=457, single run, tree-walk interpreter via
      `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18), well above the plan's 2x threshold --
      the largest ratio recorded in this series so far, expected given
      Newton's method is 40 loop iterations of interpreted arithmetic vs one
      hardware instruction. Recorded honestly, not hidden -- same
      interpreter-lane caveat as prior PERF findings (bin/simple test
      hard-defaults to the interpreter, not the JIT/native engine ordinary
      programs run on)."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering x values (seeded LCG cycling through
      0.0..1000.0 in 0.001 steps, plus forced boundary values at fixed
      moduli: 0.0 at i % 17 == 0, 1.0 at i % 13 == 0, 144.0 at i % 11 == 0,
      0.25 at i % 7 == 0), 2026-08-18: simple_us=8109 c_us=457, ratio ~17.7x.
    shared_corpus: 100
